  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n\n   The central idea is to use a sparse representation of the step function with sharp transitions, but enforce periodic structure to reduce the worst-case overlap. Unlike the parent code's smooth function, we will use a piecewise-constant function with few discontinuities. I'll implement a multi-start optimization where the starting points are derived from candidate periodic functions like sine waves and square waves, which might escape the local minima found by gradient-based methods alone.\n\n   The key hypothesis is that the optimal function for minima overlap might have a periodic component. We optimize the amplitude, frequency, and duty cycle of sine and square waves, interpolation these into fitable functions, and then apply local optimization.\n\n   The method will be computationally expensive but tractable since:\n   - We work at multiple grid resolutions (9-21 points)\n   - Each candidate comes from structured generation, reducing the search space\n\n   Different than the parent code:\n   - Combines global structure (Fourier-like) with local refinement\n   - Explicitly searches for periodic structures\n   - Uses amplitude tuning as a multi-start generator\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\n   Representation:\n   We approximate h(x) as interpolated values of a combination of sinusoidal and square functions:\n\n     h(x) \u2248 a_0 * sin(\u03c9 * x) + a_1 * cos(\u03c9 * x) + a_2\n\n   but extend to:\n\n     h(x) = f(x) clamp(0,1)\n\n   Where basis functions can be:\n     - Sine/square (periodic)\n     - Sigmoid\n     - Pulse-like\n\n   Variables:\n     Scale \u03c3 (not directly: this is for optimization)\n     Weight amplitude w (for basis functions)\n     Base value b\n     Frequency f\n     Periodicity control p\n\n   Objective:\n     C_5 = (1/(2*\u03c3)) * max_{k=-m}^{m} |\u27e8h_t, h_{t+k/\u03c3}\u27e9|   [interprets continuous overlap]\n\n   Constraints:\n     Normalize energy: integral h(x)dx = 1\n     Each h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n\n   The basis functions are randomly generated patterns with:\n     Magnitude a \u2208 [0, 0.5]\n     Base level b \u2208 [0, 0.5]\n     Frequency pulse per [0,2] interval.\n     Smoothness parameter.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\n   ```python\n   def generate_periodic_candidate():\n      # 1. Random parameters:\n      a = 0.4 * np.random.rand()\n      b = 0.1 + 0.8 * np.random.rand()\n      omega = 2 * np.pi * (0.5 + np.random.rand())\n      phase = np.random.rand() * 2 * np.pi\n\n      # 2. Build candidate function\n      if np.random.rand() < 0.5:\n          # Sine wave with square dominance near boundaries\n          h_base = np.sin(omega * x - phase)\n      else:\n          # Pulse train\n          pass\n   ```\n\n   Due to complexity constraints, we provide only the structure and its components:\n\n   Steps:\n   - Generate 5-10 candidates from oscillatory functions\n   - Evaluate each candidate at coarse grid (15-26 points)\n   - Project to satisfy 0\u2264h\u22641 and normalize area\n   - Refine grid (minimum 50 points) and optimize using the parent's methods\n\n   Grid interpolation must maintain:\n      sum(h) prop grid size\n      h clamp to [0,1]\n\n   Better peak reduction but potentially smoother function than amplitude-only search.\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n\n   Default parameters:\n      - Spline interpolation between samples\n      - Objective window: \u00b13 grid points\n      - \u03b1 in smooth_obj: 10^6\n      - Optimization modes: L-BFGS-B then SLSQP polishing\n\n   When to expand grid:\n      - When improvement rate < 0.5%\n      - When plateau detected\n\n   Fallback:\n      - Linear interpolation from neighbors\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\n   Stage resources (2 CPUs):\n      - Initial sample generation: 0.3s/board\n      - Linear interpolation: negligible\n      - Optimization per grid: 20-40s (use \u03b1=10^4 for rough)\n      - Total phase: 2-5 minutes\n\n   For 5 candidates:\n      5 * (generation 0.3 + 20s) = 5 * 20.3 = 102s\n\n   Contingency:\n      - Reduce \u03b1 if values explode\n      - Time crunch: fewer candidates\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\n   Must pass:\n     - 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 for all i\n     - sum(h)\u22480.5*n\n     - C_5 = max(np.correlate(h,1-h,axis=0)* (2/n)\n\n   Format:\n     - First deliverable: h at final grid\n     - Second: C_5 computed using the evaluator\n     - Third: final grid size n_points\n\n   The returned h will satisfy:\n      np.max(h)\u22481\n      np.min(h)\u22480\n      np.median(h or very skew)\u22480.5",
